{{also|Coral}}
==English==
[[Image:Eunicella cavolinii.jpg|thumb|{{taxlink|Eunicella cavolini|species|'''Eunicella''' cavolinii}} (1), (2)]]

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|fro|coral}} ({{cog|fr|corail}}), from {{der|en|la|corallium}}, from {{der|en|grc|κοράλλιον||coral}}. Probably ultimately of {{der|en|sem|-}} origin,<ref>{{R:grc:Lewy|pages=18–19}}</ref> compare {{cog|he|גּוֹרָל||small pebble|tr=goral}}, {{cog|ar|جَرَل||small stone}}, originally referring to the red variety found in the Mediterranean. Since ancient times, a common [[folk etymology]], accepted by some earlier scholars, connected the word instead to {{noncog|grc|κόρη}} (referring to [[Medusa]]).<ref>See e.g. ''Lithica'' (one of the [[w:Orpheus#Orphic poems and rites|Orphic poems]]), 510-610, and {{w|Pliny the Elder}}, [[w:Natural History (Pliny)|''Natural History'']], book XXXII, line 11.</ref><ref>C. W. King, ''The Natural History of Gems or Decorative Stones'', 1867, Bell & Daldy, London, pp. 100–101.</ref><ref>Liddell and Scott, ''A Greek-English Lexicon'', Harpers & Brothers, New York, 1846, p. 792.</ref> Beekes mentions both theories and considers the Semitic one convincing.<ref>{{R:grc:Beekes}}</ref>

